<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sviatoslav's invasion of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/968">968</a>–<a href="/years/971">971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was the campaign of 968–971 in which Sviatoslav I of Kiev, first invited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, conquered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> and settled at Pereyaslavets on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> then drove him out, besieging him at Dorostolon in 971; Sviatoslav was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> on his way home in 972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sviatoslavs-invasion-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Sviatoslav's invasion of Bulgaria" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Constantine Manasses · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:63-manasses-chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A treaty of 927, sealed by the marriage of Tsar Peter I to Maria, granddaughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a>, had brought almost forty years of peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, with an annual tribute paid to the Bulgarian ruler. Meanwhile Byzantium grew stronger through its wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. In late 965 or early 966 Emperor Nikephoros II Phokas refused the tribute, had the Bulgarian envoys beaten and sacked some border forts. Busy in the East and wary of Bulgaria's mountains and forests, he sent the patrikios Kalokyros to induce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a>, ruler of the Rus', to attack from the north, with a payment of 1,500 pounds of gold according to Leo the Deacon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Rus' crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, defeated a Bulgarian army of 30,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Silistra</a> and occupied most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>. Peter I retired to a monastery and died in 969, and Boris II became tsar. After driving off a Pecheneg attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> returned in force in summer 969, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> by storm and kept Boris as a figurehead, while many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> joined his army. In early 970 the Rus' crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a> and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a>, where Leo the Deacon says 20,000 survivors were impaled, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-skleros">Bardas Skleros</a> routed them near Arcadiopolis with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> week of 971 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>, who had succeeded the murdered Nikephoros on 11 December 969, led 30,000 to 40,000 men through the unguarded passes and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> on 13 April, capturing Boris II. Sviatoslav had 300 Bulgarian nobles executed and fell back on Dorostolon. After a three-month siege and three pitched battles, the last in late July, the Rus' capitulated; Byzantine chroniclers say only 22,000 of an original 60,000 remained. They were allowed to leave without their captives and plunder, swearing never again to attack imperial territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">Tzimiskes</a>'s triumph in Constantinople Boris was stripped of his insignia and the Bulgarian crown was dedicated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>. Preslav was renamed Ioannopolis, the Bulgarian patriarchate was reduced to an archbishopric, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a>. Imperial control outside the eastern cities remained largely theoretical, and in Macedonia the Cometopuli revived the Bulgarian state, whose ruler Samuel was crowned tsar in 997. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sviatoslav's invasion of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
